    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Federal People's Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> was proclaimed on 29 November 1945 after an election in which the People's Front took an average of 85% of the vote; it was renamed the Socialist Federal Republic in 1963.</li><li>Expelled from the Cominform on 28 June 1948, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> steered between the blocs: it refused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> alike, co-founded the Non-Aligned Movement and introduced workers' self-management in June 1950.</li><li>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>'s death on 4 May 1980 debt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and nationalism overwhelmed a collective presidency; the League of Communists dissolved in January 1990 and four republics declared independence between June 1991 and April 1992.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Cominform chose 28 June 1948 to expel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>: the anniversary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> Field in 1389, the assassination of Archduke Ferdinand at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> in 1914 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidovdan-constitution">Vidovdan Constitution</a> of 1921.</p></aside>
    </section>

