a/one-party-state">one-party state</a> of six people's republics.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1948</span><span class="rf-text">On 28 June the Cominform expels <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> for 'nationalist elements' and 'Trotskyism'; Soviet troops mass on the Hungarian border and 16,000 suspected Cominformists go to Goli Otok.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1950</span><span class="rf-text">Workers' self-management is introduced: workers' councils manage enterprises and share their profits.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1963</span><span class="rf-text">A new constitution renames the state the Socialist Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>; emigration of workers is legalised and Western travel opens.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1974</span><span class="rf-text">The third constitution gives the republics and the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> wide autonomy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> is elected President-for-life.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1980</span><span class="rf-text">On 4 May <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> dies; a collective presidency rotating among the republics takes his place as the foreign debt mounts.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 1990</span><span class="rf-text">The League of Communists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> dissolves along federal lines; multi-party elections bring separatist parties to power outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and Montenegro.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1992</span><span class="rf-text">On 27 April the Federal Council adopts the Constitution of the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and Montenegro, formally ending the breakup.</span></li></ol>
