    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito.jpg" alt="Josip Broz Tito" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Josip_Broz_Tito_uniform_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> invaded the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> on 6 April 1941 and had occupied and carved it up by 17 April. Two resistances arose: the royalist Yugoslav Army in the Homeland and the Communist Partisans under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a>, a pan-Yugoslav movement preaching the 'brotherhood and unity' of the Yugoslav nations. Its political body, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-fascist-council-for-the-national-liberation-of-yugoslavia">Anti-Fascist Council for the National Liberation of Yugoslavia</a> (AVNOJ), first met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihac">Bihać</a> on 26 November 1942. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a>, from 21 to 29 November 1943, the AVNOJ's second session founded Democratic Federal Yugoslavia, a federation of six equal South Slav republics, named Tito prime minister and Marshal, and left the question of monarchy to a referendum.</p><p>The Tito–Šubašić Agreement of 17 June 1944 won the exiled king's recognition and a coalition government, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> was liberated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in October 1944; by the end of April 1945 the whole country was free. In the first months after the war the Partisans executed alleged collaborators without trial until the autumn of 1945; the remaining 200,000 ethnic Germans lost their property and were interned before expulsion, and Italians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and Istria were killed in the foibe massacres or fled. The election of 11 November 1945, fairly voted but preceded by banned newspapers and threats, gave the People's Front an average of 85%; on 29 November the Constituent Assembly abolished the monarchy and proclaimed the Federal People's Republic of Yugoslavia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The war had killed over a million people, left 3.5 million homeless, wrecked 289,000 businesses, a third of industry and every mine. Work <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> of the young rebuilt under the slogan 'No rest while we're rebuilding!', aided by $553.8 million in foreign help. A Five-Year Plan of 1947 on the Soviet model built heavy industry and arms factories; between 1953 and 1960 industrial production grew 13.83% a year. After the break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> the economy took its own path: in June 1950 workers' self-management gave elected councils control of enterprises and their profits, and the reforms of 1965 ended most state control of prices and wages and allowed small businesses of up to five employees. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> grew 6.1% a year into the early 1980s and illiteracy fell from one in two in 1945 to 20% in 1961.</p><p>Prosperity rested partly on open borders. Three million foreign tourists visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> in 1965 and 4.75 million in 1970, bringing $275 million, a tenth of federal revenue, by 1969; emigration was legalised in 1963, and by the early 1970s 1.1 million workers, a fifth of the labour force, worked abroad, two thirds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>. The regions diverged: in 1965 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>'s income per head was 177.3% of the national figure and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>'s 38.6%. The oil shock of 1973–74 was met with borrowing that grew 20% a year; by the late 1980s unemployment passed 17%, real incomes had fallen 19.5%, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> reached 2,700% in 1989 and the external debt $17 billion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The armed forces were the Yugoslav People's Army (JNA), the Territorial Defence, Civil Defence and, in wartime, the police. All men served twelve months, with no alternative service and harsh treatment for objectors. Born of the Partisans, the army pursued self-sufficiency: by the mid-1950s virtually all its weapons were made at home, and its arms industry exported to non-aligned states and others such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. After the Cominform expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1948 Soviet units massed on the Hungarian border and the Hungarian army grew from 2 to 15 divisions; the security service UDBA under Aleksandar Ranković crushed Cominformist plots, 55,600 party members were expelled and about 16,000 were sent to the camp on Goli Otok. American weapons arrived from 1951, but Yugoslavia refused to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1953.</p><p>The Soviet invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in August 1968 convinced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> that Yugoslavia would be next. His doctrine of 'all-people's defence' planned a fighting retreat into the interior and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war: in 1969 the forces were reorganised into 250,000 professional soldiers, 250,000 reservists and territorial defence forces of the six republics numbering some 900,000 men. By arming the population and giving each republic its own force, the reform later played a major part in the breakup, when the army factionalised along ethnic lines and by 1991–92 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> made up almost the whole of it while the seceding states raised their own.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/politburo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/politburo.jpg" alt="Politburo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Execute_346_Politburo_passes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Politburo of the Central Committee of the All-Union Communist Party (Bolsheviks), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> was a one-party socialist federation ruled by the Communist Party of Yugoslavia, renamed the League of Communists of Yugoslavia at its Sixth Congress on 2 November 1952 and made a federation of six republican parties. Constitutions came in 1946 (amended 1953), 1963 and 1974; the Federal Assembly, entirely Communist, did little more than approve the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a>'s decisions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>, elected President-for-life in 1974, was succeeded in 1980 by a collective presidency whose chair rotated among the republics. The six republics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, Macedonia, Montenegro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, were constitutionally equal; Serbia alone contained two autonomous provinces, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>, which the 1974 constitution gave voting power equal to the republics', to the lasting anger of Serbian officials.</p><p>The regime was somewhat more humane than other Communist states but kept absolute power. Censorship was enforced by the republics, so an article banned in one could appear in another; a 1973 law restricted the media, and by 1975 Yugoslavia held 4,000 political prisoners, more than any European state but Albania and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. The Croatian Spring of 1971 was purged, and the dissident journal Praxis closed. Women gained the vote in 1945, Article 24 of the 1946 constitution declared their equality, and the Women's Antifascist Front campaigned against the full veil until it was banned in the 1950s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivo-andric"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivo-andric.jpg" alt="Ivo Andrić" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ivo-andric">Ivo Andrić</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S._Kragujevic,_Ivo_Andric,_1961_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stevan Kragujević, CC BY-SA 3.0 rs</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>'s break with Stalin in 1948 was the first open rupture in the Eastern Bloc. Having liberated itself with little Soviet help, it refused subordination, answered the Cominform's charges of 'Trotskyism' and 'nationalist elements' and built a socialism of its own, 'Titoism', with workers' self-management as its hallmark. Refusing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> alike, it became a founding member of the Non-Aligned Movement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> made 169 official visits to 92 nations and carried the Algerian FLN's demands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>.</p><p>Culturally the country belonged to the West more than to the Bloc: Western films and music were everywhere, foreign productions were shot on its locations, the 'black wave' of novels and films showed a corrupt and dehumanising society, and by the mid-1980s its rock scene was counted among the richest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivo-andric">Ivo Andrić</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> for Literature in 1961; Yugoslavia entered the Eurovision Song Contest the same year and won it in 1989 with Riva's 'Rock Me'. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> hosted the 1984 Winter Olympics, Yugoslav basketball won world championships in 1970, 1978 and 1990 and Olympic gold in 1980, water polo took Olympic gold in 1968, 1984 and 1988, and Red Star <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> won the European Cup in 1991. The memory of the joint state, its stability, free travel, education and welfare, survives as Yugo-nostalgia, and the ties among its heirs are called the 'Yugosphere'.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic.jpg" alt="Slobodan Milošević" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Slobodan Milošević</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stevan_Kragujevi%C4%87,_Slobodan_Milo%C5%A1evi%C4%87,_portret.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stevan Kragujević, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> died on 4 May 1980, aged 87, and the weakened federal system could not cope with what followed. Debt relief arranged by the 'Friends of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>' in 1983 and 1984 did not stop unemployment and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> soaring. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> demanded a republic from the protests of 1981, and Serb opposition to the provinces' autonomy grew. In 1987 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Slobodan Milošević</a> came to power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and by populist means gained de facto control of Kosovo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> and Montenegro, opposed by the leaders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. The League of Communists dissolved along federal lines in January 1990, and in that year's first multi-party elections ethnic separatist parties won everywhere but Serbia and Montenegro.</p><p>Between June 1991 and April 1992 four republics declared independence: Croatia and Slovenia on 25 June 1991, Macedonia on 25 September 1991 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> on 3 March 1992. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> recognised Croatia and Slovenia, but the status of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> outside Serbia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> outside Croatia was unresolved, and the Yugoslav Wars of 1991–2001 followed, most severely in Croatia, Bosnia and Herzegovina and Kosovo. On 27 April 1992 the Federal Council adopted the Constitution of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia for Serbia and Montenegro, formally ending the breakup; in September 1992 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> refused to treat it as the continuation of the SFRY; the Badinter Commission found that Yugoslavia had dissolved rather than suffered secessions.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Socialist Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>'s federation of six South Slav republics proclaimed in 1945, broke with Stalin in 1948 and built a socialism of workers' self-management, open borders and non-alignment that made it the most prosperous and liberal of the Communist states, until debt, nationalism and the loss of its leader in 1980 dissolved it in the Yugoslav Wars by 1992.</em></p>

